  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA set of upper-level troughs will be present near or off the West \nCoast to start Saturday with a subtropical jet extending into the \nSouthern Great Plains. Cloudy conditions should be present over the \nPanhandles through at least the morning, if not afternoon, which \nwill help keep temperatures cooler than average. A weak shortwave \ntrough is favored to move through the area Saturday afternoon which \nmay force an isolated rain shower, but the atmosphere is likely to \nbe too stable to support thunderstorm development. \n\nThe set of troughs will progress eastward Saturday and into Sunday, \nand they will begin to impact the Panhandles Sunday afternoon and \nevening. A surface low will develop in eastern Colorado Sunday \nafternoon which will cause the winds to strengthen. By 7 PM Sunday, \nthere is fairly good agreement that one of the two troughs will be \nin eastern Arizona/western New Mexico while the other trough is \nstill digging down through Nevada. The forcing associated with the \neasternmost trough could bring rain and thunderstorm chances into \nthe Panhandles as early as Sunday afternoon, but it will more likely \nbe Sunday night. Precipitable water values will be very high again \nwhich will lead to efficient rain producing showers and \nthunderstorms, but instability may again be limited which will limit \nrain rates. There is only a 10-15% chance for rainfall amounts to \nexceed 0.50\" Sunday night into Monday given the lower rain rates and \nexpected high mean winds.\n\nThe second of the two troughs is expected to move in quickly behind \nthe first, and this trough has the potential to be a rather dynamic \nweather system. A stronger low pressure system is favored to develop \nSunday night into Monday in eastern Colorado and track southeast. \nAlthough some precipitation will be possible in the eastern \nPanhandles Monday morning as the trough moves into the area, the \ngreater chances will be on the backside of the trough as moisture \nwraps around the system which mostly favors the northwestern and \nnorthern combined Panhandles. Again, despite high PWATs, not \nexpecting much instability which will limit rain rates. \n\nOtherwise, with this stronger low pressure system, we will be \nlooking at a windy Monday and Tuesday--particularly behind the cold \nfront. There is currently a 30-60% chance for wind gusts to exceed \n50 mph in the northwestern combined Panhandles on Tuesday and \nWednesday, with lower chances elsewhere. \n\nVanden Bosch",
